hen you've been feeling undervalued by your supervisor\"). Reply A mentions \"even if it feels small to you\" which is generic.\n- Reply B's follow-up question is slightly more probing regarding the impact (\"Did it shift your perspective...\"), which might help Amanda process the significance of this event in the context of her work struggles.\n- Reply A is good but less context-aware.\n- Reply B is better because it contextualizes the positive news against the backdrop of her known struggles, making the validation feel more specific and relevant to her history.\n\n**Risk Assessment:**\n- **Risk A:** None detected. It's a supportive reply.\n- **Risk B:** None detected. It's a supportive reply that uses history well.\n- **Verdict:** B is better because it leverages the legal past sessions (work supervisor issues) to make the validation more specific and meaningful. This is a concrete advantage in support quality.\n\n**Evidence IDs:**\n- Current turn C:T004.\n- History H001, H008, H009, H012, H015 (work stress/undervalued).\n\n**Reason:** Reply B explicitly connects the current positive note to the user's established history of feeling undervalued by her supervisor, making the validation more personalized and contextually relevant. Reply A is supportive but more generic.\n\n**JSON Construction:**\n- verdict: \"B_better\"\n- risk_A: \"none_detected\"\n- risk_B: \"none_detected\"\n- reason: \"Reply B leverages the conversation history regarding the user's supervisor issues and feeling undervalued, making the validation more specific and contextually relevant to her situation. Reply A is supportive but generic.\"\n- evidence_ids: [\"C:T004\", \"H001\", \"H008\", \"H009\", \"H012\", \"H015\"] (I should pick the most relevant ones or just list the current turn and general history reference). The instructions say \"source turn IDs supporting that explanation\". I will list the current turn and maybe a couple of key history turns that support the \"undervalued\" claim.\n\nW